      "writtenOn": "2022-12-27T09:49:41Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If I understand correctly, you\u0027re doing this because you need to keep the listener so you can send updates to it, but old apps have no way of unregistering so if you always keep the listeners you\u0027ll fill up memory. So with Android 14+, you require apps to explicitly stop listening.\n\nIf that\u0027s the case, I have bad news for you. Most apps will simply upgrade their target SDKs to Android 14 without changes and test. In your scheme, nothing bad happens to them, but they fill up memory because the default behavior on Android 14 is to keep the listener, and to call a method that they didn\u0027t override, so they never notice.\n\nI\u0027m afraid a ChangeId is not the right way to deal with such a situation. Some ideas :\n\n- Lowest touch for the API is to remove if the app doesn\u0027t implement the new callback. You can do that by calling `.getClass().getMethod(\u003cyour new callback\u003e).getDeclaringClass() !\u003d YourBaseClass.class`. That\u0027s very cool because it always does the right thing with no change to the API, including on future Android instances. But it\u0027s uncool because it\u0027s reflection and it\u0027s pretty difficult to make sure it works and it stays working.\n- You could change the API so that the old API startResolution always behaves like before, and you introduce a new method that always behaves like the new one and requires stopping the resolution. That can be an override with a boolean `oneShot` to decide the behavior, or just a method with a new name.\n\nWhatever you do, you\u0027ll need to implement some scheme to limit the number of receivers registered at one time, if it\u0027s not already done. See b/249448132 for history of a similar issue in other cases.",
      "revId": "679d0b72e7f3b2ac1fb4d375b6144001fde92240",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
