      "writtenOn": "2022-12-27T09:49:41Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Well too bad, you\u0027re modifying the underlying list in setHostAddress. Example code :\n\n    final NsdServiceInfo nsi \u003d ...\n    final List\u003cInetAddress\u003e prevAddresses \u003d nsi.getHostAddresses();\n    // prevAddresses.add(linkAddress); // would crash, good\n    nsi.setHostAddresses(listOf(linkAddress));\n    final List\u003cInetAddress\u003e newAddresses \u003d nsi.getHostAddresses();\n\nAfter this code, newAddresses and prevAddresses have the same content : the one that was set. This is because `unmodifiableList` doesn\u0027t return a copy ; it returns a wrapper over the existing list. So when you mutate the underlying list in l.121, you\u0027re also mutating what was returned before.\n\nThe only defense against this is defensive copies. Either you make a copy in getHostAddresses, or you make the field mutable and make a copy in setHostAddresses.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 116,
        "startChar": 27,
        "endLine": 116,
        "endChar": 43
